// `for` walks a range, binding each value in turn. A range written `..` stops
// before its upper bound and one written `..=` includes it, a difference worth
// being deliberate about every time a loop is written.

void main() {
    // A factorial multiplies every number from 1 up to and including n, so the
    // inclusive form is the one that matches the definition.
    final var n = 10;
    var result = 1;
    for (var i : 1..=n) {
        result *= i;
        print($"${i}! = ${result}");
    }

    // The same bounds written both ways, to make the difference concrete.
    // `print` ends its line, so each row is collected into a `String` first.
    var exclusive = "1..5  gives ";
    for (var i : 1..5) {
        exclusive += $"${i} ";
    }
    print(exclusive);

    var inclusive = "1..=5 gives ";
    for (var i : 1..=5) {
        inclusive += $"${i} ";
    }
    print(inclusive);

    // The loop variable belongs to the loop: it is bound afresh on each pass
    // and is gone once the loop ends. To count in a way the body controls,
    // use the `while` loop from the previous lesson instead, or the classic
    // three-part `for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)` whose step you write yourself.

    // Factorials outgrow their type quickly. 20! still fits in the 64 bits
    // behind `int`, but 21! does not. Going past the top of a type is an
    // overflow: a debug build stops with a panic and a release build wraps
    // around (Semantics §S.2), so neither gives the right answer. Growth this
    // fast is worth remembering before choosing a type.
}
